  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Stage Capabilities — HTTP fetch of staged payload", "technique": "T1608.001", "what_happened": "At 2026-11-04 09:12:08 CET, Suricata fired 'ET WEB_CLIENT Executable Download from Staging Server' against internal host wafaawindows (10.108.57.45). The alert shows data.srcip=10.108.57.45 initiating an outbound HTTP GET to data.dstip=185.220.101.47 on port 80 with data.url=/payloads/update_stage2.exe. The User-Agent is data.http.user_agent='PowerShell/7.3 WindowsPowerShell/1.0', confirming PowerShell as the downloader. Custom Wazuh rule 92600 also fired: 'PowerShell WebRequest to non-corp domain + .exe extension in URI'.", "chain_reason": "Suricata signature 'ET WEB_CLIENT Executable Download from Staging Server' (signature_id 2030052) + Wazuh rule 92600 on data.srcip=10.108.57.45 → data.dstip=185.220.101.47. The .exe extension in the URI and a PowerShell User-Agent confirm the endpoint is actively staging a PE payload.", "first_seen": "2026-11-04T09:12:08+0100"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Stage Capabilities — PE payload dropped to disk", "technique": "T1608.001", "what_happened": "At 09:13:41 CET, Wazuh FIM reported a new file created: syscheck.path='C:\\Users\\wafaawindows\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\update_e7f3a2\\svchost_upd.exe'. The FIM event type is 'added'. This path matches the %LOCALAPPDATA%\\Temp\\update_*\\ staging pattern used by the attacker. The filename svchost_upd.exe is a masquerading indicator — it mimics the legitimate Windows svchost.exe to avoid suspicion. Wazuh rule 92601 fired simultaneously: 'PE header in HTTP response body', confirming the downloaded file is a valid Portable Executable (MZ header present).", "chain_reason": "Wazuh FIM (syscheck.event=added, syscheck.path=*\\Temp\\update_*\\*.exe) + rule 92601 'PE header in HTTP response body'. The file creation timestamp lags the HTTP GET by 93 seconds, consistent with PowerShell write-to-disk latency after a completed download.", "first_seen": "2026-11-04T09:13:41+0100"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Execution — Staged payload launched from Temp", "technique": "T1204.002", "what_happened": "At 09:14:55 CET, Sysmon Event 1 on wafaawindows captured process creation: data.win.eventdata.image='C:\\Users\\wafaawindows\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\update_e7f3a2\\svchost_upd.exe' launched by parentImage='C:\\Windows\\System32\\WindowsPowerShell\\v1.0\\powershell.exe'. The commandLine contains no arguments, consistent with a dropped-and-executed PE rather than a script. Launching an executable from %LOCALAPPDATA%\\Temp is a high-confidence malware execution indicator — no legitimate software installs and self-executes from this path.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 1: parentImage=powershell.exe + image path under *\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\update_*\\*.exe + rule.mitre.id=T1204.002. The parent-child relationship (PowerShell spawning a Temp-resident .exe) matches the staged-payload-execution pattern precisely.", "first_seen": "2026-11-04T09:14:55+0100"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Outbound C2 beaconing attempt", "technique": "T1071.001", "what_happened": "At 09:15:19 CET, Suricata fired 'ET MALWARE Generic HTTP C2 Beacon' for a second HTTP connection from 10.108.57.45 to the same external IP 185.220.101.47, now on port 443. The URL is /check-in with a short POST body. This is the payload calling home after execution — the standard stage-2 C2 check-in pattern: download payload → execute → report back to staging/C2 server.", "chain_reason": "Suricata 'ET MALWARE Generic HTTP C2 Beacon' on data.srcip=10.108.57.45 → data.dstip=185.220.101.47:443 firing 24 seconds after the payload execution event. The same external IP is both the staging server (step 1) and the C2 endpoint (step 4) — single-server dual-role staging + C2, a common red-team and malware-operator pattern.", "first_seen": "2026-11-04T09:15:19+0100"}
  ],
